extract_pdf_content expalnation:
Most PDF parsers just dump text in a long string. This script is "Smart" because it looks at the visual structure of the paper:

Heading Detection: It calculates the "Body Font Size." If it sees text that is larger (e.g., 11pt vs 9pt), it marks it as a Heading. This allows it to know when a new section (like "Methodology") begins.

Logical Boundaries: It splits text at double newlines (\n\n) first (paragraphs), then at periods (sentences). It explicitly avoids cutting in the middle of a sentence.

Context Injection: It doesn't just store "Chunk 2." it prepends a header so the vector "knows" it belongs to the Transformer paper's Results section.

A. The "Smart Chunking" Engine (smart_chunk)
This function is the heart of your text processing.

It tries to fit as many full paragraphs as possible into a 400-word limit.

If a paragraph is giant (over 400 words), it switches to sentence-level splitting.

No Overlap: It relies on the Context Header to bridge the gap between chunks instead of repeating text.

B. The Multimodal Linker (find_caption_near_image)
This is where the "Multimodal" part happens.

Extraction: It uses PyMuPDF to find images on a page.

Spatial Search: It looks in a "band" (80 pixels above and below the image) to find text that looks like a caption (starting with "Figure" or "Fig.").

Pairing: It saves the image to a folder and links that specific file path to the caption text in a FigureRecord.

Intutuion in qdrant vector database:

How Point A(text chunk) and Point B(Image+caption) coexist
When you set up your Qdrant collection, you define two "slots" for vectors. One is 768-dim (for text) and one is 512-dim (for images).

Point A (Text Chunk)
Slot 1 ("text_vec"): Filled with the 768-dim BGE vector.

Slot 2 ("image_vec"): zero vector

Payload: Contains the text of the section and the section name.

Point B (Figure)
Slot 1 ("text_vec"): Filled with the 768-dim BGE vector (of the caption).

Slot 2 ("image_vec"): Filled with the 512-dim CLIP vector.

Payload: Contains the caption text and the image_path to the original file.

The full picture of how search works with this design:
User types a text query
        ↓
embed with BGE → 768-dim vector
        ↓
search text_vector slot
        ↓
Point A scores 0.87  ← text chunk, relevant
Point B scores 0.74  ← figure, caption matched
Point A scores 0.71  ← another text chunk
        ↓
return all three — both types surface together


User uploads an image query
        ↓
embed with CLIP → 512-dim vector
        ↓
search image_vector slot
        ↓
Point A scores 0.01  ← zero vec, never surfaces
Point B scores 0.91  ← figure, visually similar
Point B scores 0.83  ← another figure
        ↓
return only figures — text chunks filtered out automatically


If you're asked this in an interview, here is a professional, three-part answer that covers the math, the practical reality, and the specific architecture of your project:

## 1. Directional Meaning over Magnitude
"In my project, I used **Cosine Similarity** because it focuses on the **angle** between vectors rather than their length. In research papers, sections vary significantly in length. If I used **Euclidean distance**, a 400-word passage about 'Attention' might be seen as mathematically 'far away' from a 50-word query about 'Attention' simply because the longer text has larger raw numbers. Cosine Similarity is **scale-invariant**, meaning it finds the semantic topic regardless of how many words are used."

## 2. Alignment with Model Training
"Most modern text models, like the **BGE** model I used, are specifically trained using a loss function that optimizes for cosine similarity. Using a different metric like Euclidean distance during search would create a 'mismatch' between how the AI learned the data and how the database retrieves it, leading to lower accuracy."

## 3. Dot Product vs. Cosine Similarity
"Technically, since I **normalized** my vectors to a length of 1 in my embedding script, Cosine Similarity and **Dot Product** are mathematically identical ($A \cdot B = \cos \theta$ when $\|A\| = \|B\| = 1$). I chose the Cosine metric in Qdrant because it provides a standard, interpretable score between -1 and 1, making it easier to set confidence thresholds for the RAG system."

---

### Comparison Summary for the Interviewer

| Metric | Why I DIDN'T choose it |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Euclidean** | It measures distance like a ruler. It would punish long research sections just for having more words (magnitude). |
| **Dot Product** | It is sensitive to vector length. Unless the data is perfectly normalized, longer text would appear 'more important' than short text. |
| **Cosine** | **(Selected)** It measures the 'topic direction'. It is the industry standard for high-dimensional semantic search. |



> **Interview Tip:** If they ask "What about the 'Curse of Dimensionality'?", you can mention that as dimensions increase (like your 768-dim BGE vectors), Euclidean distance becomes less effective because the distance between points starts to look almost the same. Cosine similarity remains much more stable in high-dimensional spaces.

Algorithm,Strategy,Best For...,Why not for us?
IVF,Clustering,Large datasets with limited RAM.,We have plenty of RAM for 13k points.
PQ,Compression,Billion-scale datasets.,Compression would lose the fine detail of your research data.
LSH,Hashing,"Simple, low-dim data.",Struggles with the complexity of 768-dim BGE vectors.
HNSW,Graphs,High-accuracy RAG.,(Selected) Best balance of speed and accuracy for your scale.


